<<~ ahu #/head>>

! EventStore ~ what a vessel remembers of its own capabilities

Keyhive hands every op to a handler the moment it fires — a prekey rotation, a CGKA operation, a
delegation, a revocation. The store keeps them so a vessel that reboots can replay itself back into
the state it held.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! The invariants

# ''Replay restores; it never recomputes.'' On boot the store lists what it holds and hands the bytes
back to Keyhive to ingest. Nothing here interprets an op.
# ''Causality needs no sorting HERE.'' Keyhive tracks it internally, so a store that ordered events by
its own clock would impose an order the protocol never asked for.
# ''An event carries the island it belongs to, and the cut respects a ratchet.'' A per-island slice
stays decryptability-safe precisely because the boundary follows the ratchet rather than convenience.
# ''An event with NO island reads CROSS-CUTTING and co-loads with every slice.'' A prekey rotation
belongs to a principal rather than a document; loading one island's slice without it would leave that
island unable to read what the rotation already changed.
# ''Where an event's island comes from differs by kind, and each answer stands local'': a delegation
or revocation takes it from its own subject bytes; a CGKA operation borrows the active document
operation's island under the single-writer handler.
# ''A slice request that names no island returns EVERYTHING.'' The narrowing stays opt-in, so a
caller that has not reasoned about slices gets the whole record rather than a silent subset.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

